      <p>On 16/07/1969, Monitoring Patrol P51 reported observing an object exiting the atmosphere of Aion-354 on a collision course with the planet's moon. Over the following 75-hour mission, it was confirmed that the object was a spacecraft powered by a primitive rocket engine as it entered lunar orbit.</p>



      <p>For the next 101 hours, the craft performed a series of maneuvers, eventually detaching a capsule that descended onto the lunar surface, from which two PSC-7463 entities emerged. Analysis of their actions suggested that both individuals were conducting various experiments, collecting samples, and documenting the mission with images.</p>



      <p>Once the departure of the two PSC-7463 entities was confirmed, a reconnaissance team was dispatched to the moon of Aion-345, where they verified that the remaining spacecraft at the landing site met basic standards for early space travel engineering. In the years following this event, a significant increase in the number of PSC-7463-launched space missions has been observed, believed to be preliminary plans for colonizing other planets within the solar system.</p>



